## adv-souda-appearance (13 October 2026)
The unrestricted i-adjective deletion rule omits ない → なさそう and the lexical exception かわいい + そう, whose ordinary resulting word means pitiable rather than seems cute. The stored おいしそう, 降りそう and よさそう examples are sound, but the rule can generate wrong extrapolations. (13 October 2026)
Correction: Add ない → なさそう for the adjective of absence, and explicitly distinguish かわいそう from かわいいようだ. Do not extrapolate this adjective rule to all verb-negative variants. (13 October 2026)
Evidence: https://imabi.org/seem-%ef%bd%9e%e3%81%9d%e3%81%86%e3%81%a0/ (13 October 2026)

## adv-adjective-sa (13 October 2026)
The simple remove-i instruction would predict いさ from いい. The lesson does not depend on grammar-i-adjectives, which already documents the よい stem, so the required exception is not supplied by its declared prerequisites. The three stored examples are correct. (13 October 2026)
Correction: State the irregular base いい/よい → よさ and link grammar-i-adjectives as a prerequisite. (13 October 2026)
Evidence: https://imabi.org/adjective-nominalization/ https://www.coelang.tufs.ac.jp/mt/ja/gmod/contents/explanation/030.html (13 October 2026)

## adv-kuseni (13 October 2026)
Example 1 translates 教えてくれません as an intentional refusal. The negative benefactive says the person does not tell me; くせに supplies criticism but does not prove a deliberate refusal. This matters when the French prompt is used as an exact recall target. (13 October 2026)
Correction: Use: Il le sait, pourtant il ne me le dit pas. Keep the note that the construction expresses a reproach. (13 October 2026)
Evidence: https://imabi.org/criticizing-%e3%81%8f%e3%81%9b%e3%81%ab%e3%83%bb%e3%81%8f%e3%81%9b%e3%81%97%e3%81%a6/ https://www.coelang.tufs.ac.jp/mt/ja/gmod/contents/explanation/086.html (13 October 2026)

